WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.381
A payments company runs a database leader in three regions.

2
00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:11.247
Each takes its own customers' writes, and sends every change to the other two.

3
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:13.822
This is all-to-all replication.

4
00:00:13.822 --> 00:00:18.456
Today the link from Europe to Asia is slow: 150 milliseconds.

5
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:24.369
At time zero, Europe inserts a payment as authorized, and sends the insert to both.

6
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:31.101
The US gets it at 40 milliseconds, and at 45 it captures the payment: an update to captured.

7
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.011
It sends the update on.

8
00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:36.284
Its link to Asia takes only 30 milliseconds.

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.525
So at 75 milliseconds, the update reaches Asia before the insert.

10
00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:43.148
Asia has no such row.

11
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:49.837
pgactive, an active-active extension for PostgreSQL, discards such an update by default.

12
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:52.844
At 150 milliseconds the insert arrives.

13
00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:55.051
Europe and the US say captured.

14
00:00:55.051 --> 00:00:59.537
Asia says authorized, and nothing left in flight can repair it.

15
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.612
Timestamps don't help either.

16
00:01:01.612 --> 00:01:07.767
If the US clock runs 60 milliseconds behind, the capture gets an earlier stamp than the insert.

17
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:11.849
Keep the later stamp, and every region throws the capture away.

18
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.772
That rule, keep the later stamp, is called last write wins.

19
00:01:15.772 --> 00:01:19.416
It also settles two regions editing the same row at once.

20
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.124
Europe adds hold h-eu to merchant M-0042.

21
00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:29.743
Ten milliseconds later, before that crosses the ocean, the US adds hold h-us.

22
00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:31.204
Both are told OK.

23
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.072
The changes cross, and each leader keeps the newer one.

24
00:01:35.072 --> 00:01:39.477
Both end with h-us, and hold h-eu is gone, with no error anywhere.

25
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:46.713
In the lesson's lab, with writes 50 milliseconds apart, 38.5% of acknowledged holds vanish.

26
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:53.691
With many leaders, a change can arrive before the change it depends on, and a clock can't put them back in order.
